  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.069,
            0.816,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Predicting What Comes Next: Exploring Sequences and Progressions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "181"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.108,
            0.427,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This may be rewritten as"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.343,
            0.13,
            0.691,
            0.149
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "1 , 1 + 1 \\times 4 , 1 + 2 \\times 4 , 1 + 3 \\times 4 , \\ldots"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.153,
            0.799,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If we treat these expressions as the terms of a sequence, we get"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.279,
            0.175,
            0.756,
            0.196
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_5.jpg",
          "content": "t_{1} = 1, t_{2} = 1 + 1 \\times 4, t_{3} = 1 + 2 \\times 4, t_{4} = 1 + 3 \\times 4"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.198,
            0.884,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "and so on. We may therefore write the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " term as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { \\mathrm { n } } = 1 + ( n - 1 ) \\times 4 ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " which simplifies to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { \\mathrm { n } } = 4 n - 3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.238,
            0.886,
            0.338
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The first six terms of the sequence representing the number of squares in Fig. 8.3 are 1, 5, 9, 13, 17, 21. We note that the difference between successive terms is the constant value 4. Such sequences, in which the difference between consecutive terms is constant, are known as arithmetic progressions. We will refer to them as APs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.359,
            0.399,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.396,
            0.855,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider all the sequences we have discussed so far in this chapter. Which ones are arithmetic progressions and which ones are not? Can you justify your claim?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.478,
            0.886,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that in the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " term "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t_{n}=1+(n-1)\\times 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the sequence above, 1 is the first term of the sequence and 4 is the ‘common difference’. Similarly, the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " term of the sequence 1, 4, 7, 10, … is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t_{n}=1+(n-1)\\times3,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " where 1 is the first term and 3 is the common difference. In the sequence 11, 7, 3, –1, – 5, … the numbers decrease by 4. This is also an arithmetic progression, where the first term is 11 and the common difference is – 4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.614,
            0.826,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In general, an arithmetic progression (AP) can be described as"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.636,
            0.728,
            0.655
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_12.jpg",
          "content": "a , a + d , a + 2 d , a + 3 d , . . . , a + ( n - 1 ) \\times d ,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.656,
            0.886,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ { \\circ } a ^ { \\prime }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "first term",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ \\circ d"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "common difference",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Thus "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t_{n}=a+(n-1)\\times\\underline{d}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an expression for the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " term of any arithmetic progression, for some fixed values of a and d."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.737,
            0.404,
            0.76
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8.4.1 Visualising an AP"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.767,
            0.885,
            0.808
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us return to the growing pattern of squares in Fig. 8.3 and prepare a table that shows the number of tiny squares at each stage."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.818,
            0.881,
            0.883
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 16,
              "bbox": [
                0.117,
                0.818,
                0.881,
                0.883
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_table_16.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>Stage Number</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>…</td><td>n</td></tr><tr><td>Number of squares</td><td>1</td><td>5</td><td>9</td><td>13</td><td>17</td><td>…</td><td>4n - 3</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
